Blocking vs Non Blocking (IPC_NOWAIT)

	* Sender (msgsnd)

		If the message queue is full, msgsnd() blocks by default
		To avoid blocking, use IPC_NOWAIT

	* Receiver (msgrcv)

		If a message of the requested type is not available, msgrcv() blocks by default
		To avoid blocking, use IPC_NOWAIT

msgtyp Behavior in msgrcv()

	1. msgtyp = 0

		msgrcv (id, &msg, size, 0, flags);

			* The first message in the queue is read 
			* Message type is ignored 

	2. msgtyp > 0 

		msgrcv (id, &msg, size, msgtyp, flags);

			* Reads the first message of type = msgtyp 

		with MSG_EXCEPT 

			msgrcv(id, &msg, size, msgtyp, MSG_EXCEPT);

			* Reads the first message whose type is not equal to msgtyp 
			* Skips message of the given type 
	
	3. msgtyp < 0 (priority based receive) 

		msgrcv(id, &msg, size, -N, flags);
		
			* Reads the first message with the lowest type 
			* Message type must be <= |msgtyp|

			Example : 

				Queue Contents (type -> data)

					[ 2:hello ] → [ 1:hai ] → [ 3:bye ] → [ 2:abc ]
					
				msgrcv(id, &msg, size, -3, 0);

					* Absolute value of -3 is 3 
					* Eligible messages types: 1, 2, 3
					* Lowest type among them is 1
					* messsage received : "hai"

			If msgtyp is negative, the first message with the lowest type ≤ |msgtyp| is received.

		


















					
			
					












	
